ML_PAGE("l6-prep", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l6\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">LECTURE 6 · MCQ PREPARATION</span><h2>Lecture 6 MCQ Prep</h2><p>The complete MCQ preparation notes in their original order: 24 multiple-choice questions (4 of them coding questions) sit inside the sections they belong to, with answers hidden until you reveal them. Every question also has a <b>harder version</b> on the same concept, one reveal below its answer.</p></header><div class=\"study-grid three-col\"><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>24</strong><span>MCQs in the notes, each with a harder version</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>4</strong><span>Coding questions</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>Bloom challenge questions</span></div></div><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l6\" data-quiz=\"6\">Start Lecture 6 simulator</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"6\" data-quiz-mode=\"hard\">Hard mode</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"6\" data-quiz-mode=\"difficult\">Bloom challenge</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l6\" id=\"l6p-toc\"><summary><strong>MCQ notes</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">8 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"Lectures 6-7 - MCQ Exam Preparation\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l6p-s0\" title=\"Lectures 6-7 - MCQ Exam Preparation\">MCQ overview</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">LECTURE 6 - Supervised Learning, Regression and Tree-Based Models</li><li data-full=\"1. What you should know instantly\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l6p-s2\" title=\"1. What you should know instantly\"><span class=\"toc-no\">1</span>Know instantly</button></li><li data-full=\"Part A - Supervised Learning and Regression\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l6p-s3\" title=\"Part A - Supervised Learning and Regression\">Supervised learning and regression</button></li><li data-full=\"Part B - Logistic Regression\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l6p-s4\" title=\"Part B - Logistic Regression\">Logistic regression</button></li><li data-full=\"Part C - Decision Trees and CART\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l6p-s5\" title=\"Part C - Decision Trees and CART\">Decision trees and CART</button></li><li data-full=\"Part D - Random Forest\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l6p-s6\" title=\"Part D - Random Forest\">Random forest</button></li><li data-full=\"Part E - Lecture 6 Coding Questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l6p-s7\" title=\"Part E - Lecture 6 Coding Questions\">Coding questions</button></li><li data-full=\"Lecture 6 - Answer Key\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l6p-s8\" title=\"Lecture 6 - Answer Key\">Answer key</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"prep-notes theme-l6\" open><summary><div><strong>Lecture 6 MCQ preparation</strong><small>Know-cold tables, traps, practice questions and coding questions - section by section, answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body compact\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>8 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l6p-s0\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Lectures 6-7 - MCQ Exam Preparation</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Supervised Learning</h4>\n<p>I'll follow the same format as Lecture 5: topic-by-topic MCQs, scenario-based questions, a few coding questions, answer keys, and explanations of the most important exam traps.</p>\n<p>The combined lecture deck covers eight major algorithms. We'll separate them as follows:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Lecture 6</th>\n<th>Lecture 7</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Supervised Learning</td>\n<td>Support Vector Machine (SVM)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Linear Regression</td>\n<td>Naïve Bayes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Multiple &amp; Polynomial Regression</td>\n<td>K-Nearest Neighbours (KNN)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Logistic Regression</td>\n<td>Artificial Neural Networks (ANN)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Decision Trees &amp; CART</td>\n<td>Activation functions &amp; backpropagation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Random Forest</td>\n<td>XOR and overfitting</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>You'll get 52 questions in total, including 8 coding questions.</p></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l6p-d1\">LECTURE 6 - Supervised Learning, Regression and Tree-Based Models</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l6p-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">1. What you should know instantly</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Immediate answer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Supervised learning</td>\n<td>Learn from labelled examples</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Classification</td>\n<td>Predict categorical labels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Regression</td>\n<td>Predict numerical values</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Linear Regression</td>\n<td>$\\hat y=mX+c$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Residual</td>\n<td>Actual - Predicted</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ordinary Least Squares</td>\n<td>Minimize sum of squared residuals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Multiple Regression</td>\n<td>Two or more predictors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Polynomial Regression</td>\n<td>Include polynomial terms such as $X^2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Underfitting</td>\n<td>Model is too simple</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Overfitting</td>\n<td>Learns training-specific noise</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Logistic Regression</td>\n<td>Classification using estimated probabilities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sigmoid</td>\n<td>Maps values between 0 and 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Decision Tree</td>\n<td>IF-THEN splitting rules</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gini impurity</td>\n<td>Measures class mixture</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CART</td>\n<td>Classification and Regression Trees</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Random Forest</td>\n<td>Ensemble of Decision Trees</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bootstrap sampling</td>\n<td>Random sampling with replacement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Random feature subsets</td>\n<td>Encourage diversity among trees</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Random Forest classification</td>\n<td>Voting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Random Forest regression</td>\n<td>Averaging</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l6p-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part A - Supervised Learning and Regression</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><div><p>What makes a machine-learning algorithm supervised?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It learns without target labels</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It learns from input examples with known target outcomes</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It updates only through rewards and penalties</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It automatically selects the most important features</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H1</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A team trains a model on past customer records that include each customer's actual outcome (churned or stayed), then uses it on new customers whose outcome is unknown. Which part of this setup makes it <strong>supervised</strong> learning?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The new customers have no labels, so the model has to discover groups by itself.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The model uses many input features.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The training examples carry known target outcomes, and the model learns to map inputs to them.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A human checks some predictions after deployment.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Supervised learning means learning from <strong>labelled examples</strong>: inputs paired with known targets. A describes the prediction stage - new data is always unlabelled - and also describes clustering. B is true of most models. D is monitoring, not the learning paradigm.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><div><p>A company wants to predict the number of electricity units consumed tomorrow, measured in kWh.</p>\n<p>Which problem type is this?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Classification</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Clustering</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Regression</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Reinforcement learning</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H2</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A retailer builds two models: (1) the number of units a store will sell tomorrow (0, 1, 2, ..., 500) and (2) whether tomorrow's demand will be High, Medium or Low. Which labelling is correct?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> (1) regression, (2) classification</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Both regression, because both are about demand</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Both classification, because units sold are whole numbers</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> (1) classification, (2) regression</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A <strong>numerical quantity</strong> (units sold) is a regression target even when the values are integers; a choice among <strong>categories</strong> (High / Medium / Low) is classification. B judges by topic instead of target type. C confuses \"whole number\" with \"category\". D swaps them.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><div><p>Which scenario is an example of classification?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Predicting tomorrow's temperature</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Predicting a house's selling price</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Predicting monthly revenue</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Predicting whether a customer will default on a loan</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H3</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Which task is <strong>classification</strong>, even though its target is stored as numbers?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Predicting a patient's blood pressure in mmHg</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Predicting how many hours a student will sleep</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Predicting delivery time in minutes</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Predicting a customer's risk band coded 1 = Low, 2 = Medium, 3 = High</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The codes 1, 2, 3 are just <strong>labels for categories</strong> - the model chooses a class. A, B and C are continuous quantities (regression). The question type depends on what the target means, not on whether it is stored as a number.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><div><p>A trained regression model produces:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\hat y=5X+40$$</div>\n<p>What does the coefficient 5 represent?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The predicted output when $X=0$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The increase in predicted $y$ for a one-unit increase in $X$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The average prediction error</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The probability that the prediction is correct</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H4</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A used-car model predicts $\\widehat{Price}=50{,}000-2{,}500\\times Age$. Car A is 3 years older than car B. What does the model predict?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Car A is RM2,500 cheaper.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Car A is RM7,500 cheaper.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Car A is RM7,500 more expensive.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Nothing can be said without both cars' exact ages.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The slope is the change in prediction <strong>per one-unit increase</strong> in X: $3\\times(-2{,}500)=-7{,}500$. A uses only one year. C ignores the negative sign. D: for a straight line, the difference depends only on the gap in X, not on the starting age.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><div><p>Using the same model:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\hat y=5X+40$$</div>\n<p>What is the predicted score for a student who studies 6 hours?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 46</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 65</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 70</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 75</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p><strong>Working - Linear prediction</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\hat y=5(6)+40$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{70}$$</div></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H5</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Using $\\hat y=5X+40$ (marks from study hours), how many hours does a student need for a <strong>predicted</strong> score of 80?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 16</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 24</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 9</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 8</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Solve $80=5X+40$: $X=(80-40)/5=8$. A divides 80 by 5 and forgets the intercept. B adds the intercept instead of subtracting it. C is an arithmetic slip.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><div><p>A regression model predicts 70 marks, but the actual examination score is 75.</p>\n<p>Using:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Residual=Actual-Predicted$$</div>\n<p>what is the residual?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> -5</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> +5</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> +145</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p><strong>Working - Residual</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$e=75-70=\\boxed{+5}$$</div>\n<p>Positive residual here means the model underpredicted.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H6</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A model predicts 70 marks for two students. Student P actually scores 62 and student Q scores 75. Which statement is correct?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> P's residual is -8 (the model over-predicted); Q's is +5 (the model under-predicted).</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> P's residual is +8 and Q's is -5.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Both residuals are positive because errors are distances.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> P's residual is -8 and Q's is +5, so the model is more accurate for P.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>$Residual=Actual-Predicted$: $62-70=-8$ and $75-70=+5$. A negative residual means the prediction was <strong>too high</strong>. B uses Predicted - Actual. C confuses residuals with absolute errors. D: |-8| &gt; |5|, so the model is <strong>less</strong> accurate for P.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q7</span><div><p>A regression model produces two residuals:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$e_1=3,\\quad e_2=-4$$</div>\n<p>What is the Sum of Squared Errors (SSE)?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 1</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 7</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 12</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 25</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p><strong>Working - SSE</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$SSE=3^2+(-4)^2$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=9+16=\\boxed{25}$$</div>\n<p>Squaring prevents positive and negative errors from cancelling.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H7</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Model 1 has residuals 3, -3, 3, -3. Model 2 has residuals 5, 0, 0, 0. Which has the lower SSE, and why can't the plain sum of residuals decide?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Model 1, because its residuals sum to 0.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Model 2, because its SSE is 5 and Model 1's is 0.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Model 2 (SSE 25 vs 36); Model 1's residuals sum to 0 only because positive and negative errors cancel - which is why they are squared.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Model 1 (SSE 12 vs 25).</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Model 1: $4\\times3^2=36$. Model 2: $5^2=25$. A raw sum hides errors by cancelling them (A), which is exactly why least squares <strong>squares</strong> the residuals. B sums the residuals instead of their squares. D sums the absolute values for Model 1.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q8</span><div><p>A model predicts car CO₂ emissions using both engine volume and vehicle weight.</p>\n<p>This is an example of:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Simple Linear Regression with one predictor</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Multiple Regression</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Logistic Regression</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Unsupervised clustering</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H8</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A multiple regression $CO_2=c+m_1(Volume)+m_2(Weight)$ has $m_1=0.008$ per cm³ and $m_2=0.0075$ per kg. Two cars have the <strong>same engine volume</strong>, but car A weighs 1,000 kg more. What does the model predict?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Car A emits 8 more units, using the volume coefficient.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Car A emits 7.5 more units - with volume fixed, only the weight term changes.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Car A emits 15.5 more units, because both coefficients apply.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> No comparison is possible, because a model with two predictors is nonlinear.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Each coefficient is the change in prediction for one extra unit of <strong>that</strong> predictor, holding the others fixed: $1{,}000\\times0.0075=7.5$. A uses the wrong coefficient. C changes volume as well, which did not happen. D: multiple predictors do not make a model nonlinear - it stays linear in its coefficients.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q9</span><div><p>A relationship between input $X$ and target $Y$ follows a curved pattern.</p>\n<p>Which model can explicitly represent this using terms such as $X^2$?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Simple Linear Regression without feature transformations</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Polynomial Regression</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Naïve Bayes</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> KNN classification</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H9</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A polynomial regression is $\\hat y=2+3X-0.5X^2$. Compute the predictions at $X=2$ and $X=4$. Which statement is correct?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Both are 6: the curve rises and then falls, which a single non-flat straight line cannot do - and the model is still linear in its coefficients.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 6 and 14, so the model is a straight line.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Both are 6, so polynomial regression is a nonlinear model that least squares cannot fit.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 8 and 6.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>$2+6-2=6$ and $2+12-8=6$ (with a peak of 6.5 at $X=3$). The $X^2$ term bends the curve, yet $\\hat y$ is still a weighted sum of $1, X, X^2$, so it is <strong>linear in the coefficients</strong> and fitted the same way as linear regression. B forgets the squared term. C draws the wrong conclusion from the right numbers. D is an arithmetic slip.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q10</span><div><p>A degree-15 polynomial fits the training observations almost perfectly but performs badly on unseen data.</p>\n<p>What is the most likely problem?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Underfitting</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Overfitting</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Insufficient dimensionality</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Label encoding</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H10</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Three polynomial degrees were compared:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Degree</th>\n<th>Training error</th>\n<th>Validation error</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>40</td>\n<td>42</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>12</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>15</td>\n<td>0.5</td>\n<td>95</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Which degree should be chosen, and what describes the others?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Degree 15 - it has the lowest training error.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Degree 1 - it is the simplest, and its errors are close together.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Degree 3 - it has the lowest validation error; degree 1 underfits (both errors high) and degree 15 overfits (tiny training error, huge validation error).</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Any of them - on average they perform the same.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The degree should be chosen by <strong>validation performance</strong>, not training accuracy. Degree 1 has a small gap but large errors (underfitting). Degree 15 memorizes the training points (overfitting). A rewards memorization. B ignores that both of degree 1's errors are high. D is false.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<p>The regression portion of the lecture covers Linear, Polynomial and Multiple Regression, including residuals, least squares and the risk of fitting increasingly complex polynomial curves.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l6p-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part B - Logistic Regression</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q11</span><div><p>Despite its name, Logistic Regression is primarily used in this lecture for:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Clustering</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Dimensionality reduction</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Binary classification</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Predicting continuous prices</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H11</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Why is plain linear regression a poor choice for a 0/1 fraud label, and what does logistic regression change?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Linear regression cannot use more than one feature.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Logistic regression predicts continuous prices more accurately.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Linear regression always outputs exactly 0 or 1.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A straight line can output values below 0 or above 1, which cannot be probabilities; logistic regression passes the linear score through the sigmoid to keep the output between 0 and 1.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Logistic regression is a <strong>classification</strong> method: the sigmoid turns $z=w_1X_1+\\cdots+b$ into a probability. A is false (multiple regression exists). B misstates its purpose. C is the opposite of the problem - a line's outputs are unbounded.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q12</span><div><p>The sigmoid function is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sigma(z)=\\frac{1}{1+e^{-z}}$$</div>\n<p>What is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sigma(0)$$</div></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.25</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.5</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 1</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p><strong>Working - Sigmoid at zero</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sigma(0)=\\frac{1}{1+e^0}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\frac12=\\boxed{0.5}$$</div></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H12</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Given $\\sigma(2)\\approx0.88$, what is $\\sigma(-2)$?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> -0.88</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.12, because $\\sigma(-z)=1-\\sigma(z)$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.5</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 0.88, because the sigmoid gives the same value for $z$ and $-z$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>$\\sigma(-2)=\\frac{1}{1+e^{2}}\\approx0.12=1-0.88$ - the curve is symmetric about the point (0, 0.5). A is impossible: sigmoid outputs are always between 0 and 1. C is $\\sigma(0)$. D confuses this point symmetry with mirror symmetry.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q13</span><div><p>A Logistic Regression model predicts:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P(Fraud)=0.80$$</div>\n<p>The classification threshold is 0.5.</p>\n<p>What is the predicted class?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Legitimate, because 0.80 is below 1</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Fraud, because 0.80 exceeds the threshold</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Regression, because the output is numerical</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Uncertain, because Logistic Regression cannot classify</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H13</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A logistic model uses $z=-4+0.05\\times Amount$ and a 0.5 threshold. From what transaction amount does it start predicting Fraud?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM80, because $\\sigma(z)\\ge0.5$ exactly when $z\\ge0$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM90, because $z$ must reach 0.5</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> There is no single amount - the threshold applies to probabilities, not amounts</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM160, where $P(Fraud)$ reaches 1</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>$\\sigma(0)=0.5$, so the boundary is at $z=0$: $-4+0.05A=0\\Rightarrow A=80$. B confuses the probability threshold with a value of $z$. C: the sigmoid increases with $z$, so a probability threshold corresponds to one amount. D: the sigmoid never reaches 1.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<p>The lecture introduces Logistic Regression for binary outcomes and uses the sigmoid function to obtain values between 0 and 1.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l6p-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part C - Decision Trees and CART</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l6_tree\"><div class=\"vis-title\">How a decision tree chooses its splits</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Score one split with weighted Gini, let the tree search for the best threshold, then grow a whole CART tree.</figcaption></figure></div><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q14</span><div><p>In a Decision Tree, what is the function of a leaf node?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Represent the first decision in the tree</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Represent the final prediction or terminal outcome</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Store the entire training dataset</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Calculate the distance between observations</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H14</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A <strong>regression</strong> tree's leaf contains three training houses priced RM400,000, RM420,000 and RM440,000. A new house reaches this leaf. What does the tree predict?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM400,000, the cheapest house in the leaf</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The class \"Expensive\"</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM420,000 - a regression leaf predicts a number, commonly the average training target in that region</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Nothing yet - the leaf measures distances to the three houses first</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Leaves hold the <strong>final prediction</strong>: a majority class in a classification tree, and a number (usually the mean, here $1{,}260{,}000/3$) in a regression tree. A picks one value. B treats it as classification. D describes KNN, not a tree.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q15</span><div><p>A classification node contains:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">5 Pass\n5 Fail\n</code></pre>\n<p>Using:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Gini=1-\\sum_i p_i^2$$</div>\n<p>what is its Gini impurity?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.25</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.5</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 1</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p><strong>Working - Balanced Gini</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Gini=1-(0.5^2+0.5^2)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\boxed{0.5}$$</div>\n<p>For binary classification, this is the maximum possible Gini impurity.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H15</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A node holds 4 Red, 4 Green and 4 Blue observations. What is its Gini impurity?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.5, because Gini can never exceed 0.5</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 1, because the classes are perfectly mixed</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.333</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 0.667</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>$1-3\\times(1/3)^2=1-1/3\\approx0.667$. 0.5 is the maximum only for <strong>two</strong> classes (A); with more classes, a perfectly mixed node has higher impurity. B: Gini approaches 1 only with many equally likely classes. C is $\\sum p_i^2$, which you must subtract from 1.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q16</span><div><p>A Decision Tree node contains:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">70% Class A\n30% Class B\n</code></pre>\n<p>Calculate its Gini impurity.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.21</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.42</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.58</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 0.70</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p><strong>Working - Unequal proportions</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Gini=1-(0.7^2+0.3^2)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=1-(0.49+0.09)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{0.42}$$</div></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H16</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Node X has 8 Yes and 2 No. Node Y has 6 Yes and 4 No. Which statement is correct?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Gini(X) = 0.32 and Gini(Y) = 0.48, so Y is purer.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Gini(X) = 0.32 and Gini(Y) = 0.48, so X is purer.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Gini(X) = 0.16 and Gini(Y) = 0.24, so X is purer.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Gini(X) = 0.68 and Gini(Y) = 0.52, so X is less pure.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>X: $1-(0.8^2+0.2^2)=0.32$; Y: $1-(0.6^2+0.4^2)=0.48$. <strong>Lower Gini = purer</strong>, so X. A reverses the reading. C computes $p(1-p)$ instead of $2p(1-p)$. D forgets to subtract from 1.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q17</span><div><p>A proposed tree split produces two child nodes:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Child</th>\n<th>Observations</th>\n<th>Gini impurity</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>30</td>\n<td>0.4</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>What is the weighted Gini impurity of the split?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.10</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.20</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.30</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 0.40</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p><strong>Working - Weighted Gini</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Gini_{split}\n=\n\\frac{10}{40}(0)+\\frac{30}{40}(0.4)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\boxed{0.30}$$</div>\n<p>You must consider the sizes of both child nodes. Simply taking the lowest child Gini would be incorrect.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H17</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A parent node has 40 observations. Two candidate splits:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Split</th>\n<th>Child</th>\n<th>Observations</th>\n<th>Gini</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>30</td>\n<td>0.4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>20</td>\n<td>0.2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>D</td>\n<td>20</td>\n<td>0.3</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Which split would CART choose?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Split 2 - weighted Gini 0.25 versus 0.30, even though split 1 has a pure child.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Split 1 - any split that creates a pure child is best.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Split 1 - its average child Gini is 0.20 versus 0.25.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Neither - they are equal.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Split 1: $\\frac{10}{40}(0)+\\frac{30}{40}(0.4)=0.30$. Split 2: $\\frac{20}{40}(0.2)+\\frac{20}{40}(0.3)=0.25$. Lower <strong>weighted</strong> impurity wins. B ignores how many observations sit in the impure child. C takes an unweighted mean - the classic trap. D is an arithmetic error.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q18</span><div><p>What does CART stand for?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Classification and Regression Trees</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Classification Accuracy Ranking Technique</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Continuous Analysis Regression Training</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Categorical Attribute Regression Testing</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H18</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>One slide in the lecture lists \"inadequate for regression\" as a limitation of decision trees. Which response is correct?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Correct - decision trees can only classify.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Correct - the R in CART stands for \"Ranking\".</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Trees can do regression only after converting prices into price bands.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Incorrect as a blanket statement - CART means Classification and Regression Trees, and a regression tree's leaf predicts a numerical value.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The notes flag this contradiction: <strong>CART</strong> covers both tasks, and regression trees are a standard supervised method. A and C deny regression trees exist. B misremembers the acronym.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<p>The Decision Tree section explains root nodes, internal nodes, leaf nodes, Gini impurity and CART.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l6p-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part D - Random Forest</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q19</span><div><p>Five trees in a Random Forest predict:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Tree 1 → Fraud\nTree 2 → Legitimate\nTree 3 → Fraud\nTree 4 → Fraud\nTree 5 → Legitimate\n</code></pre>\n<p>What is the final classification under majority voting?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Fraud</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Legitimate</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Both classes equally</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A continuous numerical prediction</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H19</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A random-forest <strong>regressor</strong> has five trees that predict house prices of RM300k, RM320k, RM310k, RM290k and RM380k. What does the forest predict?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM310k, the middle value</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM300k - no value has a majority, so the first tree decides</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM320k - a regression forest averages the trees' predictions (a classification forest would use a majority vote)</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM380k, the highest prediction</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>$(300+320+310+290+380)/5=320$. Majority voting is for classification; regression <strong>averages</strong>. A is the median. B applies voting to numbers. D picks an extreme.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q20</span><div><p>Which combination introduces diversity into a typical Random Forest?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Identical training samples and identical features for every tree</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Bootstrap sampling and random feature subsets</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Scaling all features to zero</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Increasing the polynomial degree of each tree</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H20</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Suppose every tree in a forest is trained on the <strong>full</strong> training set and considers <strong>all</strong> features at every split. What happens?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The forest becomes more accurate, because each tree sees more data.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The trees become (nearly) identical and make the same errors, so the vote adds little beyond a single tree.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The forest becomes a regression model.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Majority voting becomes impossible.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Bootstrap samples and random feature subsets are the sources of <strong>diversity</strong>; without them every tree learns the same splits, and averaging identical mistakes does not reduce variance. A misses that the point is diversity, not data volume. C and D are unrelated.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<p>The lecture describes Random Forest as an ensemble of Decision Trees, using votes for classification and averaged predictions for regression.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l6p-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part E - Lecture 6 Coding Questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q21</span><div><p><strong>Coding - Linear Regression</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\n\nmodel = LinearRegression()\nmodel.fit(X_train, y_train)\n\npredictions = model.predict(X_test)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Which statement is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> fit() learns the relationship from training data, and predict() estimates outputs for test observations</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> fit() only scales the test data, and predict() removes outliers</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> fit() performs clustering, while predict() creates labels</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> fit() calculates feature variance without using target values</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H21</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><pre><code class=\"language-python\">model = LinearRegression()\nmodel.fit(X_test, y_test)\npredictions = model.predict(X_test)\n</code></pre>\n<p>What is wrong with this code?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Nothing - fit() and predict() are used correctly.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> predict() must be called before fit().</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> LinearRegression needs predict_proba() instead of predict().</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The model is fitted on the test data, so its test predictions no longer measure performance on unseen data; it should be fitted on X_train, y_train.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p><code>fit()</code> learns from the data it is given - here, the very rows it is then evaluated on, which gives an over-optimistic score. A misses this. B reverses the order. C: predict_proba is for classifiers.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q22</span><div><p><strong>Coding - Logistic Regression</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\n\nmodel = LogisticRegression()\nmodel.fit(X_train, y_train)\n\nprobabilities = model.predict_proba(X_test)\n</code></pre>\n<p>What does predict_proba() return in this classification context?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Numerical residuals only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Estimated probabilities for each class</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The coefficients of a polynomial regression model</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Distances to the nearest training observations</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H22</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A fraud classifier has <code>model.classes_ = [0, 1]</code> (1 = Fraud). <code>model.predict_proba(X_test)</code> returns <code>[[0.2, 0.8], [0.7, 0.3]]</code>. Which interpretation is correct?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Row 1: P(Fraud) = 0.8, so it is predicted Fraud; row 2: P(Fraud) = 0.3, so it is Legitimate. Each row sums to 1, and the columns follow <code>classes_</code>.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Both rows are Fraud, because each contains a value of at least 0.5.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The first column is Fraud, because Fraud is the more important class.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> These are residuals; positive values mean fraud.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>predict_proba returns <strong>one probability per class</strong> in the order of <code>classes_</code>, and with a 0.5 threshold the larger column decides. B ignores which class the large value belongs to. C invents a column order. D confuses classification probabilities with regression residuals.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q23</span><div><p><strong>Coding - Decision Tree</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeClassifier\n\nmodel = DecisionTreeClassifier(\n    max_depth=3,\n    random_state=42\n)\n\nmodel.fit(X_train, y_train)\n</code></pre>\n<p>What does max_depth=3 control?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The number of target classes</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The maximum depth of the Decision Tree</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The minimum training-set size of three</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The number of features retained by scaling</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H23</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>For <code>DecisionTreeClassifier(max_depth=3)</code>, which statement is correct?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The tree has exactly 3 leaves.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The whole tree may contain at most 3 splits.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The tree can split at most 3 levels below the root, so it has at most $2^3=8$ leaves; raising it to 20 lets the tree fit finer patterns and increases overfitting risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The tree may use at most 3 features.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>max_depth limits the <strong>length of the longest root-to-leaf path</strong>; each level can at most double the number of leaves. A and B confuse depth with leaves or splits. D confuses depth with features. Limiting depth is a standard way to stop a tree from memorizing the training data.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q24</span><div><p><strong>Coding - Random Forest</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier\n\nmodel = RandomForestClassifier(\n    n_estimators=100,\n    random_state=42\n)\n</code></pre>\n<p>What does n_estimators=100 specify?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Use 100 input features</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Use 100 training observations</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Build 100 decision trees in the ensemble</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Return 100 class labels</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H24</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A random forest's <code>n_estimators</code> is raised from 100 to 1,000. What is the most accurate expectation?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Each tree now receives 10 times more data.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> More trees vote or average, so predictions become more stable, while training and prediction take longer; the individual trees are no deeper.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The forest now uses 1,000 features.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The forest overfits 10 times more.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>n_estimators is the <strong>number of trees</strong> in the ensemble; combining more diverse trees steadies the result at a computational cost. A and C confuse trees with rows or features. D: adding trees does not make each tree more complex - the forest's averaging is what reduces variance.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l6p-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Lecture 6 - Answer Key</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the answer key</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Q</th>\n<th>Answer</th>\n<th>Q</th>\n<th>Answer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>13</td>\n<td>B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>14</td>\n<td>B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>D</td>\n<td>15</td>\n<td>C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>16</td>\n<td>B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>17</td>\n<td>C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>18</td>\n<td>A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>D</td>\n<td>19</td>\n<td>A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>20</td>\n<td>B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>21</td>\n<td>A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>22</td>\n<td>B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>23</td>\n<td>B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>24</td>\n<td>C</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Important calculations and explanations</h3>\n<p><strong>Q5 - Linear prediction</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\hat y=5(6)+40$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{70}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Q6 - Residual</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$e=75-70=\\boxed{+5}$$</div>\n<p>Positive residual here means the model underpredicted.</p>\n<p><strong>Q7 - SSE</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$SSE=3^2+(-4)^2$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=9+16=\\boxed{25}$$</div>\n<p>Squaring prevents positive and negative errors from cancelling.</p>\n<p><strong>Q12 - Sigmoid at zero</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\sigma(0)=\\frac{1}{1+e^0}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\frac12=\\boxed{0.5}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Q15 - Balanced Gini</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Gini=1-(0.5^2+0.5^2)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\boxed{0.5}$$</div>\n<p>For binary classification, this is the maximum possible Gini impurity.</p>\n<p><strong>Q16 - Unequal proportions</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Gini=1-(0.7^2+0.3^2)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=1-(0.49+0.09)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{0.42}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Q17 - Weighted Gini</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Gini_{split}\n=\n\\frac{10}{40}(0)+\\frac{30}{40}(0.4)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\boxed{0.30}$$</div>\n<p>You must consider the sizes of both child nodes. Simply taking the lowest child Gini would be incorrect.</p>\n<h3>Important slide warning - Gini calculation</h3>\n<p>The worked calculation on the slides of the lecture omits the leading 1 - in some of its Gini calculations. For example:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$0.2^2+0.8^2=0.68$$</div>\n<p>is the sum of squared probabilities, not Gini impurity.</p>\n<p>The correct Gini impurity is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$1-0.68=\\boxed{0.32}$$</div>\n<p>For your examination, follow the correct formula introduced on pages 35-36:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{Gini=1-\\sum p_i^2}$$</div></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"question-bank-wrap hard-wrap\" id=\"l6-hard\"><summary><div><strong>Bloom challenge - 30 questions</strong><small>18 Bloom-graded questions (3 per level) and 12 marked extension questions. Answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><p class=\"hard-intro\">Questions climb Bloom&#39;s taxonomy from <b>Remember</b> to <b>Create</b>. The <b>extension</b> questions go beyond the slides (industry, coding, hackathon, Master&#39;s, PhD and postdoc level) but can be reasoned out from the lecture&#39;s principles.</p><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn\" data-quiz=\"6\" data-quiz-mode=\"difficult\">Start Lecture 6 Bloom challenge</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"6\" data-quiz-mode=\"extensions\">Extensions only</button></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Which pairing of model and the representation it learns matches the lecture?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Linear regression → majority vote; Random Forest → straight-line equation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Logistic regression → IF-THEN rules; Decision Tree → probability function</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Linear regression → an equation; Logistic regression → a probability through the sigmoid; Decision Tree → partitions and rules; Random Forest → many aggregated trees</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Decision Tree → least-squares line; Logistic regression → averaging of trees</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The lecture's summary: <strong>equation, probability function, partitions and rules, aggregated trees</strong>. A, B and D attach each representation to the wrong model.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Which statement about Gini impurity is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $Gini=\\sum_i p_i^2$, and 1 means a pure node.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $Gini=1-\\sum_i p_i^2$; 0 means a pure node, and for two classes the maximum is 0.5 (a 50/50 split).</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Gini is the number of misclassified observations.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $Gini=1-\\sum_i p_i$; it is always 0.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Subtracting the <strong>sum of squared class proportions</strong> from 1 gives 0 when one class has $p=1$ and is largest when the classes are evenly mixed. A gives the \"purity\" sum, not impurity. C describes an error count. D: $\\sum p_i=1$, so that expression is always 0 and useless.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>What are the two sources of randomness in a typical Random Forest?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Random labels and random thresholds</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Random polynomial degrees and random scaling</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Random number of trees and random test sets</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Bootstrap samples of the training rows for each tree, and a random subset of features considered at each split</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Rows are sampled <strong>with replacement</strong> per tree, and features are sampled <strong>per split</strong>; together they make the trees diverse. A would destroy the signal. B and C are not part of the method.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Why does Ordinary Least Squares minimize the sum of <strong>squared</strong> residuals instead of their plain sum?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Squaring stops positive and negative errors from cancelling, and penalizes large errors more than small ones.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Squaring makes all predictions positive.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The plain sum is always larger.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Squaring removes outliers.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Residuals of +5 and -5 sum to 0 but represent real error; squaring gives 50. Because $10^2$ is four times $5^2$, large misses are penalized disproportionately. B confuses residuals with predictions. C is false. D: squaring actually makes outliers more influential.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>In what sense is $\\hat y=b_0+b_1X+b_2X^2$ still a \"linear\" model?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It produces a straight line.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It only works when $X$ is small.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The prediction is a weighted sum of the features $1, X, X^2$, so it is linear in the coefficients $b_0, b_1, b_2$ and can be fitted by least squares, even though the curve bends in $X$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The $X^2$ term is ignored during fitting.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>\"Linear\" in linear regression refers to the <strong>coefficients</strong>, not the shape of the curve. Polynomial regression just adds transformed columns. A is false - the curve is not straight. B and D are false.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Why is one fully grown decision tree considered unstable, and how does a forest address this?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A tree is too simple to fit any data, and a forest makes it more complex.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Small changes in the training data can change early splits and so the whole tree (high variance); averaging many decorrelated trees cancels much of that variation.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Trees cannot handle numerical features, and forests convert them.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Trees need scaled inputs, and forests scale them automatically.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A different first split cascades down the tree, so a deep tree's predictions <strong>vary a lot</strong> between samples. A forest averages many such trees trained on varied data, reducing variance. A has it backwards - deep trees overfit. C and D are false.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>For $x=[1,2,3,4]$ and $y=[2,4,5,8]$, use $b_1=\\frac{\\sum(x-\\bar x)(y-\\bar y)}{\\sum(x-\\bar x)^2}$ and $b_0=\\bar y-b_1\\bar x$. What is the fitted line?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $\\hat y=2x$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $\\hat y=1.9x+0.5$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $\\hat y=1.5x+1$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $\\hat y=1.9x$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>$\\bar x=2.5$ and $\\bar y=4.75$. Numerator: $4.125+0.375+0.125+4.875=9.5$. Denominator: $2.25+0.25+0.25+2.25=5$. So $b_1=1.9$ and $b_0=4.75-1.9(2.5)=0$. A is the slope from the end points only, $(8-2)/(4-1)$. B and C are arithmetic slips.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>A logistic model has $z=-3+0.8x_1+0.5x_2$. For $x_1=2$ and $x_2=4$, what are $P(Fraud)$ and the class at a 0.5 threshold?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> About 0.65 → Fraud</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> About 0.6 → Legitimate</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.6 → Fraud, because $z$ is the probability</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> About 0.35 → Legitimate</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>$z=-3+1.6+2=0.6$, and $\\sigma(0.6)=1/(1+e^{-0.6})\\approx0.646\\ge0.5$, so Fraud. C treats $z$ as a probability - it is a score passed through the sigmoid. D is $\\sigma(-0.6)$. B mixes the two.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>A parent node holds 10 Yes and 10 No. A split gives Left = 7 Yes / 1 No and Right = 3 Yes / 9 No. What is the weighted Gini of the split, and how much does it reduce impurity?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.297; a reduction of 0.203</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.3125; a reduction of 0.1875</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.25; a reduction of 0.25</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 0.375; a reduction of 0.125</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Parent: 0.5. Left: $1-(\\tfrac78)^2-(\\tfrac18)^2=\\tfrac{14}{64}\\approx0.219$. Right: $1-(\\tfrac{3}{12})^2-(\\tfrac{9}{12})^2=0.375$. Weighted: $\\tfrac{8}{20}(0.219)+\\tfrac{12}{20}(0.375)=0.0875+0.225=0.3125$; reduction $0.5-0.3125=0.1875$. A takes the plain mean of the two children. D uses only the right child.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>A straight-line fit leaves residuals that are positive for small X, negative in the middle and positive again for large X. What does this pattern tell you?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The model is perfect, because residuals average to zero.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The data has outliers that must be deleted.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The relationship is curved; a polynomial term such as $X^2$ (chosen by validation) is likely to help.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Logistic regression is required.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Random scatter around 0 is healthy; a <strong>systematic U-shape</strong> means the line misses a curve. This is the motivation for polynomial regression. A: OLS residuals average to zero even when the model is wrong. B: the pattern is structural, not a few points. D: the target is still continuous.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>Results on one split:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Model</th>\n<th>Train accuracy</th>\n<th>Test accuracy</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Tree, max_depth = 2</td>\n<td>74%</td>\n<td>72%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tree, unlimited depth</td>\n<td>100%</td>\n<td>78%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Random Forest, 300 trees</td>\n<td>100%</td>\n<td>88%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>What is the best analysis?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The depth-2 tree underfits; the unlimited tree overfits (high variance); the forest's trees also fit training data closely, but averaging decorrelated trees cuts the variance and lifts the test accuracy.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The forest overfits most, because its training accuracy is 100%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The depth-2 tree is best, because its gap is smallest.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Test accuracy is irrelevant when training accuracy is 100%.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Read <strong>both</strong> columns: low/low means underfitting; high/much lower means variance. A forest's 100% training accuracy is normal (individual trees are deep); the gain is in test accuracy. B judges by training accuracy alone. C ignores that both scores are low. D is backwards.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>In a logistic model, raising the amount by RM10 always adds 0.5 to $z$. How much does $P(Fraud)$ change when the starting probability is 0.5, compared with 0.99?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The same amount in both cases, because the coefficient is fixed.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> More at 0.99, because the probability is already high.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It decreases in both cases.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> About +0.12 at 0.5 (to 0.62), but only about +0.004 at 0.99 - the sigmoid is steep around 0.5 and flat near 0 and 1.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>A fixed change in $z$ moves the probability <strong>non-linearly</strong>: $\\sigma(0.5)-\\sigma(0)\\approx0.122$, while from $z=\\ln 99\\approx4.6$ the same +0.5 adds only about 0.004. The effect is constant on the log-odds scale, not on the probability scale (A). B is backwards. C ignores that the coefficient is positive.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D13</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>A bank must explain every loan decision to customers, but also wants accuracy. Validation accuracy: a depth-4 tree 84%, a Random Forest 87%. Which recommendation is most defensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Weigh it up: the depth-4 tree gives readable IF-THEN rules that meet the explanation requirement; if the 3-point gain is worth it, the forest needs extra explanation tools, and the decision should rest on the business and regulatory needs.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Always the forest - 3 points matter more than anything else.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The tree, because trees are always more accurate.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Neither - supervised learning cannot be explained.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The lecture lists <strong>interpretability</strong> as a tree advantage, while forests trade it for stability and accuracy. A good evaluation names the trade-off. B ignores a hard requirement. C contradicts the numbers. D is false.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D14</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>A student picks the polynomial degree with the highest <strong>training</strong> R²: degree 12. Which critique is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Correct - a higher R² is always better.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Training R² can only rise (or stay the same) as terms are added, so it always favours the largest degree; the degree should be chosen by validation error.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> R² cannot be computed for polynomials.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Degree 12 is fine as long as the data is scaled.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Adding terms gives least squares more freedom on the <strong>same</strong> data, so training fit never gets worse - the overfitting trap behind the degree-15 example. A ignores this. C is false. D: scaling helps numerically but does not prevent overfitting.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D15</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>A colleague claims: \"Random Forests cannot overfit, so grow every tree as deep as possible on our 200 noisy rows.\" What is the best response?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Agree - forests are immune to overfitting.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Disagree - forests can never fit noise.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Partly true - adding more trees doesn't overfit, but each deep tree can still memorize noise in a small dataset; forests <strong>reduce</strong> variance rather than eliminate overfitting, so tune <code>max_depth</code> or <code>min_samples_leaf</code> by validation.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Agree, but only if the data is scaled.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>More trees stabilize the average, but correlated deep trees fit to the same noisy patterns still generalize imperfectly. A overstates the claim. B is wrong in the other direction. D: scaling is irrelevant to trees.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D16</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>House price rises with floor area but levels off for very large houses, and also depends on age and location. Design the model.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Polynomial regression of degree 15 on all features, chosen by training error.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A simple linear regression on floor area only.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Logistic regression, then threshold the probability to get a price.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A multiple regression with floor area and floor area² (and age and location as further predictors), with the degree compared by cross-validation and evaluated on a held-out test set.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Combine <strong>multiple</strong> predictors with a <strong>polynomial</strong> term only where the curve exists, then let validation decide whether the extra term helps. A invites overfitting. B ignores both the curve and the other drivers. C is a classification method.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D17</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>A fraud team needs a probability per transaction and an alert rule that reflects that missing fraud costs far more than a false alarm. Design the approach.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Linear regression with predictions rounded to 0 or 1.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Logistic regression giving <code>predict_proba</code>; choose the alert threshold on validation data by minimising expected cost - likely below 0.5 - then confirm it on the test set.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Logistic regression with a fixed 0.5 threshold, because the lecture uses 0.5.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A Decision Tree with unlimited depth, using its training accuracy.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Probabilities plus a <strong>cost-based threshold</strong> turn the lecture's 0.5 default into a business decision; tuning on validation data keeps the test set honest. A gives outputs outside [0, 1] and no probabilities. C ignores the unequal costs. D overfits and ignores costs.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D18</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>Design a fair comparison of a single Decision Tree and a Random Forest for churn prediction.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Train both on all the data and compare training accuracy.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Tune the tree on the test set and the forest on the training set.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Use the same train/test split; tune each model's key hyperparameters (tree: <code>max_depth</code>; forest: <code>n_estimators</code>, <code>max_depth</code>, <code>max_features</code>) with cross-validation on the training set; compare once on the untouched test set.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Give the forest 100 trees and the tree <code>max_depth=1</code>, then compare.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>A fair comparison means the <strong>same data</strong>, equal tuning effort, and a single final test. A measures memorization. B uses different and leaky protocols. D handicaps one model on purpose.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension questions</span><small>beyond the notes, lowest Bloom level first</small></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Coding</span></div><p>A fitted logistic regression has a coefficient of 0.7 for <code>late_payments</code>. What does it mean?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Each extra late payment multiplies the <strong>odds</strong> of default by $e^{0.7}\\approx2.01$, holding the other features fixed.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Each extra late payment adds 0.7 to the probability of default.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The probability of default is 70%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Late payments explain 70% of the variance.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Logistic regression is linear in the <strong>log-odds</strong>: $\\ln\\frac{p}{1-p}=z$. A one-unit increase adds 0.7 to $z$, which multiplies the odds by $e^{0.7}$. B is impossible as a constant effect (probabilities are bounded - see D12). C and D are misreadings.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>How do Extremely Randomized Trees (<code>ExtraTreesClassifier</code>) differ from a Random Forest?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> They use a single tree.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> They require scaled features.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> They choose split thresholds at <strong>random</strong> (keeping the best of several random candidates) instead of searching for the best threshold, and by default train on the whole dataset without bootstrapping - more randomness, lower variance, often slightly higher bias, faster training.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> They train trees one after another, each correcting the last.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>ExtraTrees push the forest's diversity idea further, from random rows and features to random <strong>cut points</strong>. D describes boosting. A and B are false.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>For $x=[1,2,3,4]$ and $y=[2,4,5,8]$ with an intercept column, $X^\\top X=\\begin{bmatrix}4&amp;10\\\\10&amp;30\\end{bmatrix}$ and $X^\\top y=\\begin{bmatrix}19\\\\57\\end{bmatrix}$. Solve the normal equations $\\beta=(X^\\top X)^{-1}X^\\top y$.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $\\beta=(0, 1.9)$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $\\beta=(4.75, 1.9)$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $\\beta=(1, 1.5)$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $\\beta=(0.5, 1.9)$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>$\\det=4(30)-10^2=20$, so $(X^\\top X)^{-1}=\\tfrac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}30&amp;-10\\\\-10&amp;4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\beta=\\tfrac{1}{20}(30\\cdot19-10\\cdot57,\\ -10\\cdot19+4\\cdot57)=(0,1.9)$. This is the <strong>closed-form</strong> OLS solution, matching the slope/intercept formulas. B puts $\\bar y$ in the intercept. It shows why OLS has a unique answer whenever $X^\\top X$ is invertible.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Coding</span></div><p>Fit $\\hat y=wx$ to the points (1, 2) and (2, 4) by gradient descent on $L=\\tfrac1n\\sum(wx-y)^2$, starting from $w=0$ with $\\eta=0.1$. What is $w$ after one step?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.1</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 1.0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 2.0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> -1.0</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>$\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial w}=\\tfrac2n\\sum(wx-y)x=\\tfrac22[(-2)(1)+(-4)(2)]=-10$, so $w=0-0.1(-10)=1.0$. The optimum is $w=2$ (C), reached iteratively. It is the same update rule as in Lecture 7's networks: an iterative alternative to the normal equations when there are huge numbers of features.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>For a 70% / 30% node, what are the entropy $H=-\\sum p_i\\log_2 p_i$ and the Gini impurity?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Entropy 0.42, Gini 0.88</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Both 0.5</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Entropy ≈ 0.881, Gini 0.42 - both are 0 for a pure node and largest at 50/50, so they usually choose very similar splits</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Entropy 0.3, Gini 0.7</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>$-0.7\\log_2 0.7-0.3\\log_2 0.3\\approx0.881$ (maximum 1 for two classes), and Gini $=1-0.49-0.09=0.42$ (maximum 0.5). scikit-learn offers <code>criterion=\"gini\"</code> (the CART default) or <code>\"entropy\"</code>/<code>\"log_loss\"</code> (information gain). A swaps them.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>Cost-complexity pruning scores a tree as $R_\\alpha(T)=R(T)+\\alpha\\lvert\\text{leaves}\\rvert$. Tree A has error 0.10 with 20 leaves; tree B has error 0.14 with 5 leaves. With $\\alpha=0.005$, which is preferred?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Tree A (0.20 vs 0.165)</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Tree A, because its error is lower</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> They tie</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Tree B (0.165 vs 0.20) - the leaf penalty outweighs A's lower training error</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>A: $0.10+0.005\\times20=0.20$. B: $0.14+0.005\\times5=0.165$, so B wins. scikit-learn exposes this as <code>ccp_alpha</code>, usually chosen by cross-validation. It controls overfitting by <strong>pruning</strong>, as an alternative to capping <code>max_depth</code>. A computes correctly but then picks the larger score.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Hackathon</span></div><p>Short on time and data, you want a validation estimate for a Random Forest <strong>without</strong> a separate validation split. Which option works?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Report training accuracy.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <code>RandomForestClassifier(oob_score=True)</code>: each row is predicted only by trees that did not see it during training, and the result is in <code>oob_score_</code>.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Set <code>n_estimators=1</code>.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Use <code>max_depth=None</code>.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Out-of-bag predictions use the ~37% of rows each tree <strong>never trained on</strong>, giving a near-free generalization estimate (it requires <code>bootstrap=True</code>, the default). A is optimistic. C and D don't produce an estimate. Keep a final test set for the reported score anyway.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>If each tree's prediction has variance $\\sigma^2=1$ and every pair of trees has correlation $\\rho=0.3$, the average of $B$ trees has variance $\\rho\\sigma^2+\\frac{1-\\rho}{B}\\sigma^2$. What is it for $B=100$, and what happens as $B\\to\\infty$?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.01; it goes to 0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.307; it levels off at $\\rho\\sigma^2=0.3$, so reducing the correlation between trees matters more than adding trees</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.7; it grows with B</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 1; averaging never helps</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>$0.3+0.7/100=0.307$. The second term vanishes with more trees, but the <strong>correlation floor</strong> remains. That is why Random Forest samples features at each split (<code>max_features</code>): it lowers $\\rho$. A assumes independent trees. C and D misread the formula. (Hastie, Tibshirani and Friedman, <em>Elements of Statistical Learning</em>, §15.2.)</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>In a bootstrap sample of size $n$ drawn with replacement from $n$ rows, what fraction of the original rows is left out, roughly, for large $n$?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> About 36.8%, since $(1-\\tfrac1n)^n\\to e^{-1}$ - these \"out-of-bag\" rows give each tree free validation data</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 50%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0%, because $n$ rows are drawn</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 10%</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Each draw misses a given row with probability $1-\\tfrac1n$, and over $n$ draws that gives $\\approx e^{-1}\\approx0.368$. So every tree sees about <strong>63.2%</strong> of the distinct rows. C forgets the repeats. The left-out rows power the OOB error estimate.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>A credit model has 300 features, many strongly correlated. Which regularization assessment is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Ridge ($L_2$) and Lasso ($L_1$) behave identically.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Ridge zeroes features; Lasso keeps all of them.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Neither helps with correlated features.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Ridge shrinks all coefficients and spreads weight across correlated features (stable, but uses all of them); Lasso sets some exactly to zero (sparse, but may pick one of a correlated group arbitrarily); Elastic Net mixes both - tune the strength by cross-validation.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Regularization adds a penalty to OLS's SSE, trading a little bias for less <strong>variance</strong> - the same overfitting control as choosing a low polynomial degree. B swaps the behaviours. A and C are false.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>Which comparison of bagging (Random Forest) and boosting (e.g. gradient boosting) is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Both train trees independently and simply average them.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Boosting mainly reduces variance; bagging mainly reduces bias.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Bagging cannot be used for classification.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Bagging trains deep trees independently on bootstrap samples and averages them, mainly reducing <strong>variance</strong>; boosting trains shallow trees one after another, each fitting the previous errors, mainly reducing <strong>bias</strong> - and it can overfit with too many rounds, so it needs a learning rate and early stopping.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The lecture's forest is the bagging branch of ensembles; boosting is its sequential counterpart and dominates many tabular benchmarks. B swaps the roles. A and C are false.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>On a training set where one feature <strong>perfectly</strong> separates the classes, an unregularized logistic regression is fitted by maximum likelihood. What happens?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The coefficients converge to small, stable values.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The model cannot be fitted at all, because the data has no errors.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The likelihood keeps improving as the coefficient grows, so the estimate diverges towards infinity (probabilities pushed to 0 or 1); regularization such as scikit-learn's default $L_2$ penalty ($C=1.0$) keeps the coefficients finite.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The intercept becomes exactly 0.5.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>With complete separation, a steeper sigmoid always fits better, so the maximum-likelihood estimate <strong>does not exist</strong> (Albert and Anderson, 1984). Solvers report huge coefficients or convergence warnings, and the model is overconfident. A penalty fixes this, linking logistic regression to the regularization idea in X3. A, B and D are false.</p></div></details></div></details></div></div></details>");
